WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.378 --> 00:00:03.965
- Got on the elevator in L.A.
one day and the lady said,

00:00:03.965 --> 00:00:06.878
Oh, I just saw you in
the Hudsucker Reduction.

00:00:06.878 --> 00:00:10.295
I had a long haul, from where I came from

00:00:11.776 --> 00:00:13.859
to here, but here we are.

00:00:14.752 --> 00:00:17.598
Your job as an actor is to give it

00:00:17.598 --> 00:00:20.430
your best shot, every time.

00:00:20.430 --> 00:00:23.558
So, there was a period,
there in the early 80's,

00:00:23.558 --> 00:00:25.510
when I didn't get any work

00:00:25.510 --> 00:00:28.510
because I would ask these questions.

00:00:30.508 --> 00:00:33.649
- He's an American actor,
producer and narrator.

00:00:33.649 --> 00:00:37.030
He is known for his distinctively
smooth, and deep voice.

00:00:37.030 --> 00:00:39.367
He's ranked as the third
highest box office star

00:00:39.367 --> 00:00:43.105
with over 4.3 billion
dollars in box office gross.

00:00:43.105 --> 00:00:45.218
He's Morgan Freeman and
here's my take on his

00:00:45.218 --> 00:00:47.301
top 10 rules for success.

00:00:50.013 --> 00:00:52.524
- To collect unemployment,
you have to go out

00:00:52.524 --> 00:00:55.185
and hunt for employment.

00:00:55.185 --> 00:00:58.848
You have a code and
mine was a clerk typist;

00:00:58.848 --> 00:01:01.127
so, you go out and you look
for work as a clerk typist.

00:01:01.127 --> 00:01:02.794
And one day, I just,

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:09.677
I can't keep being forced
to be a clerk typist.

00:01:13.207 --> 00:01:14.568
So, I went to the employment office

00:01:14.568 --> 00:01:18.061
and I asked to see the supervisor.

00:01:18.061 --> 00:01:21.647
And I was lead into this
office, this pretty little lady,

00:01:21.647 --> 00:01:26.564
redhead, whose name was Mrs.
Lipton, what can I do for you?

00:01:26.564 --> 00:01:31.562
And I said, you're forcing
me to be something I'm not.

00:01:31.562 --> 00:01:34.203
I have to go out and look
for work as a clerk typist,

00:01:34.203 --> 00:01:37.286
I'm not a clerk typist, I'm an actor.

00:01:38.917 --> 00:01:42.250
So, I just can't keep doing it this way.

00:01:43.509 --> 00:01:47.676
She took this thing and
stamped my employment book,

00:01:50.509 --> 00:01:54.426
and said you've got six
months, go be an actor.

00:01:56.655 --> 00:01:59.488
So, if Mrs. Lipton is alive today,

00:02:00.963 --> 00:02:03.238
Mrs. Lipton, I'm an actor.

00:02:03.238 --> 00:02:05.514
The box office success when

00:02:05.514 --> 00:02:09.604
The Shawshank Redemption
came out was Dumb and Dumber.

00:02:09.604 --> 00:02:11.348
(crowd laughing)

00:02:11.348 --> 00:02:12.416
- I'm just not going to laugh.

00:02:12.416 --> 00:02:16.583
- Well, no, but that's,
you know, the juxtaposition

00:02:17.495 --> 00:02:21.662
of box office and, what's
that other term, critical.

00:02:24.603 --> 00:02:27.436
Shawshank got really good reviews,

00:02:29.694 --> 00:02:33.230
but because nobody could
say Shawshank Redemption,

00:02:33.230 --> 00:02:35.456
it didn't get word of mouth.

00:02:35.456 --> 00:02:39.200
And I don't care how
much you promote a film,

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:43.295
if you don't have word of
mouth, you aint promoting it.

00:02:43.295 --> 00:02:46.558
People have to be able
to go and say, listen,

00:02:46.558 --> 00:02:50.475
I saw this really terrific
film, it was called,

00:02:53.782 --> 00:02:56.900
(crowd laughing)

00:02:56.900 --> 00:02:57.817
Shanksham--

00:02:58.862 --> 00:03:01.612
(crowd laughing)

00:03:06.391 --> 00:03:07.296
Shimshunk--

00:03:07.296 --> 00:03:10.046
(crowd laughing)

00:03:11.755 --> 00:03:15.251
Got on the elevator in L.A.
one day and the lady said,

00:03:15.251 --> 00:03:18.267
Oh, I just saw you in
the Hudsucker Reduction.

00:03:18.267 --> 00:03:21.017
(crowd laughing)

00:03:24.455 --> 00:03:28.934
So, that was the reason it
was not a box office success.

00:03:28.934 --> 00:03:30.803
No word of mouth at all.

00:03:30.803 --> 00:03:32.919
- [Don] Here's a stat from your show,

00:03:32.919 --> 00:03:35.377
and it says that the richest
85 people on this planet

00:03:35.377 --> 00:03:38.767
have as much money as
the poorest 3.5 billion.

00:03:38.767 --> 00:03:43.098
- Yeah, right, that's
on the planet however.

00:03:43.098 --> 00:03:45.084
You know, that's--

00:03:45.084 --> 00:03:46.938
- [Don] It's not far off here in America.

00:03:46.938 --> 00:03:48.728
- No it isn't far off in America,

00:03:48.728 --> 00:03:51.395
but we still hear, don't abandon

00:03:53.125 --> 00:03:55.625
the idea that it isn't finite.

00:03:56.672 --> 00:03:58.575
It isn't finite if you were born,

00:03:58.575 --> 00:03:59.863
where were you born?

00:03:59.863 --> 00:04:00.868
- Me?
- Yeah, you.

00:04:00.868 --> 00:04:02.769
- Baton Rouge, Louisiana,
Baton Rouge General Hospital.

00:04:02.769 --> 00:04:06.936
- Baton Rouge, Louisiana, I
was born in Memphis, Tennessee.

00:04:10.002 --> 00:04:14.085
I had a long haul from
where I came from to here,

00:04:16.016 --> 00:04:17.743
but here we are.

00:04:17.743 --> 00:04:18.576
- [Don] Right.

00:04:18.576 --> 00:04:21.328
- So, proof is in, the
proof of the pudding

00:04:21.328 --> 00:04:23.644
is in the eating and here
we sit at the dining table.

00:04:23.644 --> 00:04:25.891
- But it's hard to, when
you say that to some people,

00:04:25.891 --> 00:04:27.809
because they say, well,
there you go with the pull

00:04:27.809 --> 00:04:30.755
yourself up by the bootstraps
thing, and, you know,

00:04:30.755 --> 00:04:32.401
you're just being respectable.

00:04:32.401 --> 00:04:34.568
Not everybody can do that.

00:04:35.896 --> 00:04:40.572
- Bull (beeps), everybody can.

00:04:40.572 --> 00:04:42.693
Everybody doesn't.

00:04:42.693 --> 00:04:46.276
Courage, courage is
the key to life itself.

00:04:48.191 --> 00:04:50.394
There are a lot of people
who are born in situations

00:04:50.394 --> 00:04:52.875
where they say, well, I'll
never get out of this,

00:04:52.875 --> 00:04:54.125
so, they won't.

00:04:54.988 --> 00:04:58.410
I say to people who say, well,
I would like to have done

00:04:58.410 --> 00:05:01.052
so and so, so and so, and I
say, well, you could've done it.

00:05:01.052 --> 00:05:03.150
They say, well, I couldn't
get out of here, well,

00:05:03.150 --> 00:05:05.321
man, the bus runs every day.

00:05:05.321 --> 00:05:07.571
(laughing)

00:05:09.862 --> 00:05:10.807
- You're exactly right.

00:05:10.807 --> 00:05:11.640
- Yeah.

00:05:11.640 --> 00:05:15.236
- If you can conceive it in your mind,

00:05:15.236 --> 00:05:16.574
- If you can think of it, you can do it,

00:05:16.574 --> 00:05:18.556
that's the human condition,
if we can imagine it,

00:05:18.556 --> 00:05:19.442
we can do it.

00:05:19.442 --> 00:05:20.275
- [Don] Right.

00:05:20.275 --> 00:05:25.232
- And I think that's the condition
of each individual human.

00:05:25.232 --> 00:05:29.905
Your job as an actor is
to give it your best shot

00:05:29.905 --> 00:05:34.253
every time, so, yes, I'm
going into it with the idea

00:05:34.253 --> 00:05:37.251
that I'll play it to
the best of my ability

00:05:37.251 --> 00:05:39.893
and they wouldn't give it to me, the role,

00:05:39.893 --> 00:05:43.161
if they didn't think I was
capable of pulling it off,

00:05:43.161 --> 00:05:45.245
or, I wouldn't take it if I didn't think

00:05:45.245 --> 00:05:47.703
I was capable of pulling it off.

00:05:47.703 --> 00:05:51.494
- So what advice would you
give purely in an acting sense,

00:05:51.494 --> 00:05:53.994
to yourself, 30, 40 years ago?

00:05:55.932 --> 00:06:00.099
- Same advice I gave myself
30, 40 years ago, get a job.

00:06:01.560 --> 00:06:03.810
(laughing)

00:06:05.243 --> 00:06:06.076
Act.

00:06:09.013 --> 00:06:13.131
There are, unfortunately,
those of us who say,

00:06:13.131 --> 00:06:15.298
well, I want to be a star,

00:06:20.466 --> 00:06:24.133
that's way out there
in the ether somewhere.

00:06:25.107 --> 00:06:29.900
I want to be a working
actor, you can pull that off.

00:06:29.900 --> 00:06:31.567
So, act, work, work.

00:06:34.602 --> 00:06:37.512
Someone asked me once,
and you're about to do it,

00:06:37.512 --> 00:06:39.762
(laughing)

00:06:40.708 --> 00:06:44.075
what would you do if you weren't an actor?

00:06:44.075 --> 00:06:48.242
If you didn't make it as an
actor, what would you do?

00:06:49.086 --> 00:06:52.253
I have no idea, I would act somewhere.

00:06:54.051 --> 00:06:55.505
Maybe I'd be driving a cab,

00:06:55.505 --> 00:06:58.317
maybe I'm working somebody's yard,

00:06:58.317 --> 00:07:01.572
whatever I'm doing, I'm
going to belong to somebody's

00:07:01.572 --> 00:07:04.322
little theater group, I will act,

00:07:05.806 --> 00:07:07.849
cause I'll die if I don't.

00:07:07.849 --> 00:07:10.526
I got into the air force because I was,

00:07:10.526 --> 00:07:14.693
as it turns out, I was
romanticizing that whole idea,

00:07:18.005 --> 00:07:20.585
you know, we were re-fighting World War II

00:07:20.585 --> 00:07:24.752
as I was growing up, so, we
had all these heroic movies,

00:07:26.924 --> 00:07:31.101
and I fell in love with
the idea of flying.

00:07:31.101 --> 00:07:34.518
And, then, when I got into the air force,

00:07:35.891 --> 00:07:38.639
I had this big eye opener,

00:07:38.639 --> 00:07:41.556
that racism was rampant there also,

00:07:43.483 --> 00:07:44.983
and that I didn't,

00:07:46.176 --> 00:07:50.343
I am not as qualified or as
suited, is a better word,

00:07:53.347 --> 00:07:56.014
for military life, that kind of,

00:07:59.930 --> 00:08:03.222
unquestioning obedience to a lot of things

00:08:03.222 --> 00:08:07.548
that I think are stupid,
didn't quite go over well.

00:08:07.548 --> 00:08:09.651
(laughs)

00:08:09.651 --> 00:08:13.816
So, when I got out I
was an early discharge.

00:08:13.816 --> 00:08:17.525
It was almost dishonorable you know,

00:08:17.525 --> 00:08:21.417
cause I just really didn't fit
but the catalyst, actually,

00:08:21.417 --> 00:08:24.500
was I did get to sit in a jet trainer

00:08:27.103 --> 00:08:30.323
cause I had a lot of people
behind me to get me into

00:08:30.323 --> 00:08:32.296
flight school and stuff,

00:08:32.296 --> 00:08:34.661
but sitting there, it occurred to me

00:08:34.661 --> 00:08:37.994
that this is absolutely not what I want.

00:08:39.265 --> 00:08:42.954
It isn't movies, it's for real, you know,

00:08:42.954 --> 00:08:46.538
this little red button
on the joystick controls

00:08:46.538 --> 00:08:48.782
guns with real bullets.

00:08:48.782 --> 00:08:51.949
I sat there for quite a while and knew

00:08:53.081 --> 00:08:57.248
that if I made a strafing
run, I needed a camera here,

00:08:58.442 --> 00:08:59.855
and I needed the people
to be able to get up

00:08:59.855 --> 00:09:01.431
and go back to number one.

00:09:01.431 --> 00:09:04.305
(laughs)

00:09:04.305 --> 00:09:07.888
So, I got out of that
plane, on the runway,

00:09:09.152 --> 00:09:12.687
at North Island Air Station,
a Naval air station,

00:09:12.687 --> 00:09:16.604
and I walked away from
that and towards acting.

00:09:17.624 --> 00:09:20.895
I knew that it was all about the movies.

00:09:20.895 --> 00:09:23.064
It was all about the movies.

00:09:23.064 --> 00:09:25.026
I got on stage the very first time

00:09:25.026 --> 00:09:28.774
when I was eight years
old, I was brilliant.

00:09:28.774 --> 00:09:31.679
Then, I got on stage
again four years later,

00:09:31.679 --> 00:09:35.081
when I was 12, brilliant again.

00:09:35.081 --> 00:09:37.574
So, by the time I was
13, it was pretty much

00:09:37.574 --> 00:09:40.529
a foregone conclusion in
everybody's else's mind,

00:09:40.529 --> 00:09:44.696
teachers, parents, that acting
was going to be my thing.

00:09:49.451 --> 00:09:52.784
I hadn't, like made a conscious decision

00:09:55.157 --> 00:09:56.681
that I'm going to be an actor,

00:09:56.681 --> 00:09:58.896
I don't think you do, I think you just,

00:09:58.896 --> 00:10:00.086
that's what you do.

00:10:00.086 --> 00:10:02.401
The first stage play I did in New York,

00:10:02.401 --> 00:10:05.357
I was working with Stacy
Keach in Viveca Lindfors',

00:10:05.357 --> 00:10:07.392
that was a great experience for me.

00:10:07.392 --> 00:10:11.775
You talk about a masterclass,
you have it there.

00:10:11.775 --> 00:10:14.723
Stacy had come out of
Yale University, I think,

00:10:14.723 --> 00:10:18.754
and he was much more
advanced as a stage actor

00:10:18.754 --> 00:10:21.834
than I was, but wow, did I have big ears,

00:10:21.834 --> 00:10:26.078
and was I ready to learn;
so, you just sit and watch.

00:10:26.078 --> 00:10:28.911
It's always a learning experience.

00:10:29.767 --> 00:10:33.934
The big thing I learned from
working with Pearl Bailey was

00:10:35.029 --> 00:10:38.228
professionalism, she was
a total professional,

00:10:38.228 --> 00:10:40.610
total, total, total professional.

00:10:40.610 --> 00:10:44.777
She always gave 100 percent,
110 percent in a performance.

00:10:47.940 --> 00:10:50.690
And I watched her very carefully.

00:10:52.725 --> 00:10:55.602
She was one of those performers

00:10:55.602 --> 00:10:57.776
that you say, this is the way to go,

00:10:57.776 --> 00:10:59.609
this is the way to be,

00:11:01.198 --> 00:11:02.327
in this business.

00:11:02.327 --> 00:11:05.281
Be on time, know what you're doing,

00:11:05.281 --> 00:11:09.448
do it full out, give all
you can, that's a lesson.

00:11:12.268 --> 00:11:14.964
As I said, I went to
the movies all the time.

00:11:14.964 --> 00:11:18.214
I've seen the movies, and at some point

00:11:23.230 --> 00:11:25.980
it struck me rather dramatically,

00:11:29.297 --> 00:11:31.964
how much I wasn't in the movies.

00:11:35.026 --> 00:11:39.261
Not the way I needed to be in the movies.

00:11:39.261 --> 00:11:42.661
And, if you look at a lot
of the disaster movies

00:11:42.661 --> 00:11:46.828
in that period, the only
people left on the planet

00:11:47.847 --> 00:11:50.514
were white, so, now, my thing is

00:11:52.386 --> 00:11:54.285
if I get in the movies,

00:11:54.285 --> 00:11:57.868
I want to make sure,
that I speak about that.

00:11:59.641 --> 00:12:03.794
That if I am able to do it,
I want to talk about it.

00:12:03.794 --> 00:12:06.894
So, there was a period
there in the early 80's,

00:12:06.894 --> 00:12:08.792
when I didn't get any work

00:12:08.792 --> 00:12:11.792
because I would ask these questions.

00:12:14.696 --> 00:12:18.863
I auditioned, oh, I don't
think I even got to audition,

00:12:20.515 --> 00:12:24.682
I interviewed for the movie,
The Thing, the remake.

00:12:27.240 --> 00:12:31.407
So, I read the script, and
I go back for the audition,

00:12:34.993 --> 00:12:38.389
and the producer or the
director, one of those,

00:12:38.389 --> 00:12:40.885
said, did you read the
script, I said, yes.

00:12:40.885 --> 00:12:42.682
What did you think?

00:12:42.682 --> 00:12:46.849
I said, well, you've got 11
people at the South Pole,

00:12:50.484 --> 00:12:54.651
eight of them are scientists,
and then you have a cook,

00:12:55.532 --> 00:12:59.615
a mechanic and something
else, they're all black,

00:13:02.572 --> 00:13:04.767
none of the scientists are.

00:13:04.767 --> 00:13:07.298
What do you think I think?

00:13:07.298 --> 00:13:11.465
Needless to say, I didn't
get that job. (laughs)

00:13:13.189 --> 00:13:17.364
Again though, you know, providence
rears her beautiful head,

00:13:17.364 --> 00:13:19.822
when Glory came about.

00:13:19.822 --> 00:13:23.051
I was just floored, cause I knew about

00:13:23.051 --> 00:13:27.218
the 54th Massachusetts
Regiment from a one page thing

00:13:28.960 --> 00:13:32.210
on This Day in History in Jet Magazine.

00:13:33.455 --> 00:13:36.228
She talked about this
Sergeant who was awarded a

00:13:36.228 --> 00:13:40.200
Medal of Honor for his
activities at this charge

00:13:40.200 --> 00:13:42.223
with this outfit.

00:13:42.223 --> 00:13:46.390
So, the history is there,
it's there to draw upon.

00:13:47.941 --> 00:13:52.878
But, we could ask ourselves,
we, me, black people,

00:13:52.878 --> 00:13:56.711
why aren't we, why don't
we figure more in it?

00:13:58.382 --> 00:14:01.452
I need something that's
going to engage me,

00:14:01.452 --> 00:14:04.035
that makes me sit up and think.

00:14:05.089 --> 00:14:07.261
I refuse to take part in anything

00:14:07.261 --> 00:14:10.493
that is going to denigrate a people.

00:14:10.493 --> 00:14:13.826
(soft orchestral music)

00:14:16.418 --> 00:14:17.585
Especially me.

00:14:18.631 --> 00:14:21.172
(audience cheers)

00:14:21.172 --> 00:14:24.730
- [Jimmy] Alright, here we go, ready?

00:14:24.730 --> 00:14:26.367
Do you ever want to go to space?

00:14:26.367 --> 00:14:27.899
(audience laughs)

00:14:27.899 --> 00:14:29.673
- I really shouldn't be doing this.

00:14:29.673 --> 00:14:32.256
(Jimmy laughs)

00:14:38.822 --> 00:14:42.049
- It's helium, it's helium,
Morgan, it is helium.

00:14:42.049 --> 00:14:44.049
- It's very undignified.

00:14:45.011 --> 00:14:47.978
- (laughing) I think
your voice sounds great,

00:14:47.978 --> 00:14:49.728
it's not undignified.

00:14:50.721 --> 00:14:52.132
Some people just have voices like this

00:14:52.132 --> 00:14:56.697
so you're actually making
fun of those people.

00:14:56.697 --> 00:14:57.530
- See.

00:14:57.530 --> 00:15:00.842
(audience laughs)

00:15:00.842 --> 00:15:01.759
I told you.

00:15:06.946 --> 00:15:07.779
- I want to thank you

00:15:07.779 --> 00:15:08.960
so much for being on the show,

00:15:08.960 --> 00:15:12.380
and, please, would you
ever like to come back?

00:15:12.380 --> 00:15:14.373
- I'm never coming back.

00:15:14.373 --> 00:15:15.368
- Thank you guys so much for watching,

00:15:15.368 --> 00:15:18.590
I made this video because
JP Annett asked me to,

00:15:18.590 --> 00:15:19.685
so, if there's a famous entrepreneur

00:15:19.685 --> 00:15:21.214
that you want me to profile next,

00:15:21.214 --> 00:15:22.552
leave it in the comments below,

00:15:22.552 --> 00:15:23.466
and I'll see what I can do.

00:15:23.466 --> 00:15:24.652
I'd also love to know
what you learned most

00:15:24.652 --> 00:15:26.390
from Morgan Freeman, which of the 10 clips

00:15:26.390 --> 00:15:28.394
had the biggest impact to you and why,

00:15:28.394 --> 00:15:29.979
what change are you going
to make in your business

00:15:29.979 --> 00:15:32.191
or life as a result of
watching this video?

00:15:32.191 --> 00:15:33.514
What's the impact?

00:15:33.514 --> 00:15:34.455
Leave it in the comments below

00:15:34.455 --> 00:15:35.753
and I will join the discussion.

00:15:35.753 --> 00:15:37.142
Finally, I want to give
a quick shout out to

00:15:37.142 --> 00:15:39.244
Kris Schantz from www.happyworker.com.

00:15:39.244 --> 00:15:41.131
Kris, thank you so much
for picking up my book,

00:15:41.131 --> 00:15:42.303
